WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.325
People walk in.

00:00:01.505 --> 00:00:02.630
They look around.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.105
Then… they leave.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.100
For Leo, it's becoming a familiar story.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.550
He runs a furniture shop in a

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.775
busy neighborhood.

00:00:10.825 --> 00:00:12.300
The products are ready.

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:14.200
The customers are out there.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.625
But after days of trying to bring

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.615
them back, he's still guessing.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.840
Who should he reach?

00:00:18.915 --> 00:00:20.365
And what should he offer?

00:00:20.415 --> 00:00:22.325
The clues are already there, in what

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.825
shoppers browse, what they ask about, and

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:26.200
what they buy.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.850
Leo just needs a way to connect

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.600
them.

00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:31.583
Could AI help him figure it out,

00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.608
and bring those customers back?

00:00:34.408 --> 00:00:37.683
Meet Market Metro: a simulated commerce world

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:40.783
where AI agents learn from shopper behavior,

00:00:40.858 --> 00:00:44.133
build campaigns, and show what happens next.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.415
First, we give the agent a map.

00:00:46.465 --> 00:00:47.915
That's our ontology.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.315
It connects shoppers, products, interactions, and payments

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.540
into one understandable story.

00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:55.765
It keeps a product recommendation separate from

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.790
a general inquiry, and makes sure one

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.165
purchase isn't counted twice.

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:02.365
Now the agent has a clearer picture

00:01:02.415 --> 00:01:04.690
of what happened, which examples to learn

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.440
from, and who might be ready to

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.540
return.

00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:11.092
It studies demand, checks available stock, and

00:01:11.142 --> 00:01:13.842
uses local tools to train two models.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.000
Before a campaign goes out, the verifier

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:21.975
checks the candidates against fixed rules.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.675
Which one produces better outcomes for merchants

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.950
and PayPal?

00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:29.342
Then we follow the campaign into the

00:01:29.392 --> 00:01:30.342
market.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:31.792
Day thirty five.

00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:33.992
A shopper who hasn't purchased in sixteen

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:36.442
days receives a recommendation.

00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:38.867
A ninety six dollar side table catches

00:01:38.917 --> 00:01:39.867
their interest.

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:41.017
They buy it.

00:01:41.092 --> 00:01:43.092
The next day, it arrives.

00:01:43.142 --> 00:01:45.667
Under fixed rules, that same request went

00:01:45.742 --> 00:01:46.817
unfilled.

00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:49.758
Here's where PayPal closes the loop.

00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:52.208
In our demo, that exact order connects

00:01:52.258 --> 00:01:54.783
to a PayPal Sandbox capture.

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:56.408
We can follow the purchase through the

00:01:56.458 --> 00:01:58.978
payment, the processing fee, and what the

00:01:59.028 --> 00:02:00.578
merchant receives.

00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:04.558
And across this market, the difference adds

00:02:04.558 --> 00:02:05.184
up.

00:02:05.184 --> 00:02:07.620
Sixteen more retained purchases.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.474
Over twenty three hundred dollars in additional

00:02:10.474 --> 00:02:11.866
payment volume.

00:02:11.866 --> 00:02:14.023
More than a thousand dollars in extra

00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:15.902
merchant contribution.

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.408
And roughly a hundred fifteen dollars more

00:02:18.408 --> 00:02:20.983
in modeled PayPal net, after AI cost.

00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:23.217
Want to see how we got there?

00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:24.992
Open AG Grid.

00:02:25.092 --> 00:02:27.517
Follow the models, the agent's decisions, the

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:30.142
campaign, and the PayPal receipt.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.200
Leo started with a guess.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.325
Now there's a tested campaign, and a

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.175
trail showing what worked.

00:02:36.275 --> 00:02:37.375
Market Metro.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.675
Agentic commerce that learns.
